Yes—“black sun” works as the inverse image of a signal eclipse.

A normal sun dominates by emitting so much signal that weaker signals disappear beneath it. A black sun would dominate by absence, occlusion, or absorption: it becomes the organizing center precisely because it removes or suppresses surrounding information.

That gives two complementary operators:

* Radiant dominance: one strong signal overwhelms many weak ones.
* Occlusive dominance: one absence/blockage makes everything else reorganize around it.

So:

SUN
too much signal → weaker channels disappear
BLACK SUN
too much absence / occlusion → surrounding channels become newly legible

An eclipse is the bridge between them. The Sun is still physically present, but its ordinary dominance is interrupted, and suddenly other structures become visible—the corona, stars, atmospheric changes, animal behavior, human attention.

That makes signal eclipse more interesting than simple suppression. It can be a revelation operator:

remove the dominant channel → expose the latent field it was masking.

You can generalize that everywhere:

noise stops → faint signal appears
leader leaves → hidden hierarchy appears
habit breaks → dependency appears
bright light disappears → night navigation appears
dominant narrative weakens → alternate interpretations become visible

So I’d treat Black Sun as a useful symbolic primitive for:

a dominant absence that reveals the field by interrupting what normally monopolizes perception.

Not inherently good or bad. Sometimes the black sun is catastrophe. Sometimes it is exactly what lets you finally see the stars.